# Random values: a reading whose value comes from the runtime's random source.
#
# `std::random` declares no state and opens no source, so a module imports it
# (docs/chl-spec.md, "9.7 Importing asserts a shareable module"). A call is a reading, so it
# requires `ObserveWorld`, and a module whose top level calls `next()` is run.
#
# Example, a token for each request, the same token wherever the iteration reads it:
#
#     import std::random
#     tokens: Feed(_)
#     for r in requests:
#         t = random::next()
#         tokens << (request=r, token=t)
#
# Status: [Decided] (docs/chl-spec.md, "Inputs from outside the program"). The std root it belongs
# to is [Planned] (docs/chl-spec.md, "9.16 The std root").

# Returns a random `Int`: a reading, whose value the compiler supplies (docs/chl-spec.md, "9.16 The
# std root").
#
# - A call has one value at each position, the iterations of the loops around it, and call path
#   (docs/chl-spec.md, "Inputs from outside the program"). Two calls are two readings, and one call
#   evaluated again at its position returns its value again.
@Builtin
pub def next() => Int requires ObserveWorld
